# XSH pthread_cond_clockwait(), pthread_cond_timedwait() and pthread_cond_wait()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Spurious wakeups are allowed (DESCRIPTION): every wait sits in a loop on
# its predicate, no test counts wakeups, and a 0 return is always
# accepted. Nothing bounds how soon a woken or canceled thread runs: a
# missed wakeup is a FAIL only after 10 s.

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.001
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       A thread in pthread_cond_wait() or pthread_cond_timedwait()
           (abstime 10 min ahead) on an error-checking mutex, signaled
           after the predicate is set, gets 0 and then owns the mutex:
           another thread's pthread_mutex_trylock() fails and its own
           pthread_mutex_unlock() succeeds.
test       t/001-owns-mutex.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.002
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       A signal sent by a thread as soon as it can lock the mutex
           after the waiter set its flag under that mutex and called
           pthread_cond_wait() (or pthread_cond_timedwait()) is not lost:
           the wait ends (20 rounds).
test       t/002-atomic-release.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.003
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/ETIMEDOUT
class      required
since      2008
says       On a condition variable with no clock attribute set,
           pthread_cond_timedwait() with no signal returns ETIMEDOUT for
           a CLOCK_REALTIME abstime 1 s in the past and for one 300 ms
           ahead, never before CLOCK_REALTIME reaches abstime, and the
           caller owns the (error-checking) mutex on return.
note       Zero returns before the timeout are spurious wakeups and are
           waited through; no ETIMEDOUT by 10 s past abstime is a FAIL.
           The system clock must not be stepped during the test.
test       t/003-timedwait-timeout.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.004
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       On a condition variable whose clock attribute was set to
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC with pthread_condattr_setclock(),
           pthread_cond_timedwait() measures abstime against
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC: an abstime in the past and one 300 ms ahead
           give ETIMEDOUT, never before CLOCK_MONOTONIC reaches abstime.
note       Since 2024 because CLOCK_MONOTONIC became mandatory then; the
           clock attribute itself has been in the Base since 2008.
test       t/004-clock-attribute.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.005
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       While a thread repeats pthread_cond_timedwait() with 200 ms
           timeouts and nobody signals, another thread blocked in
           pthread_mutex_lock() on the same mutex gets it within 10 s,
           and the waiter owns the mutex after its waits.
test       t/005-timeout-releases.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.006
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_cond_timedwait() with an abstime 3 s ahead whose
           tv_nsec is -1 or 1000000000 returns EINVAL, and the caller
           still owns the (error-checking) mutex.
test       t/006-einval-nsec.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.007
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/EPERM
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_cond_wait() and pthread_cond_timedwait() return EPERM
           when the caller does not own the mutex: an unlocked
           error-checking mutex (which stays unlocked), an error-checking
           mutex another thread holds (which that thread still owns
           afterwards), and an unlocked robust mutex.
test       t/007-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.008
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.2
class      required
since      2008
says       A thread blocked in pthread_cond_wait() (and, separately, in
           pthread_cond_timedwait()) that is canceled runs its cleanup
           handler, in which it owns the (error-checking) mutex, and ends
           with PTHREAD_CANCELED.
test       t/008-cancel-in-wait.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.009
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2008
says       Of two threads blocked on one condition variable, one is
           canceled and has finished; one pthread_cond_signal() then ends
           the other's wait.
note       Only the case where the signal follows the cancellation is
           tested; a signal concurrent with the cancellation cannot be
           arranged.
test       t/009-canceled-not-waiting.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.010
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2008
says       Caught signals (SIGUSR1, no SA_RESTART) sent with
           pthread_kill() to a thread in pthread_cond_wait() or
           pthread_cond_timedwait() never make the wait return anything
           but 0, and the wait still ends when the predicate is set and
           signaled.
test       t/010-signal-during-wait.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.011
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       After the only waiter on a condition variable, using mutex 1,
           has been unblocked by pthread_cond_broadcast() and returned, a
           wait on that condition variable with mutex 2 works: it returns
           0 when broadcast and the caller owns mutex 2.
test       t/011-new-binding.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.012
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/EOWNERDEAD
class      conditional
when       TSH
since      2008
says       A thread waits with a robust process-shared mutex; a child
           process locks the mutex during the wait and exits holding it;
           after a pthread_cond_signal() the wait returns EOWNERDEAD and
           the caller has the mutex (pthread_mutex_consistent() and
           pthread_mutex_unlock() succeed).
test       t/012-eownerdead.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.013
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/ENOTRECOVERABLE
class      conditional
when       TSH
since      2008
says       A thread waits with a robust process-shared mutex; a child
           process locks it and exits holding it; another thread locks it
           (EOWNERDEAD) and unlocks it without pthread_mutex_consistent();
           after a pthread_cond_signal() the wait returns ENOTRECOVERABLE.
note       If the waiter, woken spuriously, takes the mutex first after
           the owner died (EOWNERDEAD is then its own result), the try is
           repeated, up to 5 times, then UNRESOLVED.
test       t/013-enotrecoverable.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.014
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/ETIMEDOUT
class      required
since      2024
says       pthread_cond_clockwait() with CLOCK_REALTIME and with
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC, and no signal, returns ETIMEDOUT for an
           abstime in the past and for one 300 ms ahead, never before
           that clock reaches abstime, the caller owning the mutex.
test       t/014-clockwait-timeout.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.015
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2024
says       pthread_cond_clockwait() returns EINVAL for a tv_nsec of -1 or
           1000000000 (CLOCK_REALTIME and CLOCK_MONOTONIC, abstime 3 s
           ahead) and for a clock ID that clock_gettime() rejects with
           EINVAL, and the caller still owns the mutex.
note       The unknown clock is found by trying a few IDs with
           clock_gettime(); none rejected is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/015-clockwait-einval.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_cond_wait.016
anchor     functions/pthread_cond_wait.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       pthread_cond_clockwait() (CLOCK_REALTIME, then
           CLOCK_MONOTONIC, abstime 10 min ahead) signaled after the
           predicate is set returns 0 with the caller owning the mutex.
test       t/016-clockwait-signaled.c
